rsz: defer an endpoint that is not paying and revisit it after the sweep

Policy candidate from the repair-knobs campaign (ideas/repair-knobs-nihl.md),
carried while it is measured. Not upstreamed. The first candidate whose
failure mode is not "stopped before the win": it never stops, it
reorders.

The worst-first sweep spends its early passes on the endpoints hardest
to fix, and the endpoints that pay may sit behind them. On mock-alu cts
the default spends passes 300-900 on seventy endpoints at 5-25 passes
each for almost nothing, then gains 12 ns of TNS at passes 1000-1200;
every rule that stops on recent return cut before that and lost the
design. This one defers instead: an endpoint that has not improved
within the phase's own patience is put aside with its passes counted,
the sweep goes on, and the deferred endpoints get the rest of their
budget after the sweep, worst first. Every endpoint is still visited and
no endpoint gets more passes than before; the order changes, the total
does not.

Patience is the run's own: twice the median passes the improving
endpoints have needed so far, at least three, and the legacy limit until
five endpoints have improved. No value a user could set.

Chesterton's Fence: the legacy loop gives each endpoint up to fifty
non-improving passes because some endpoints need many passes before
their first improvement; deferring keeps that budget, it only spends it
later, after the endpoints that improve quickly have had their turn.

